body:
Thanks again!

I forgot about one more warning, this is the weirdest one. Only happens with Clang:

``` cpp
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/mat4x4.hpp:6:
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/detail/type_mat4x4.hpp:195:
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/detail/type_mat4x4.inl:4:
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/detail/func_matrix.hpp:150:
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/detail/func_matrix.inl:4:
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/detail/../geometric.hpp:6:
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/detail/func_geometric.hpp:114:
In file included from ../thirdparty/glm/detail/func_geometric.inl:247:
../thirdparty/glm/detail/func_geometric_simd.inl:67:87: warning: unused parameter 'I' [-Wunused-parameter]
  inline static tvec4<float, P> call(tvec4<float, P> const& N, tvec4<float, P> const& I, tvec4<float, P> const& Nref)
                                                                                      ^
1 warning generated.
```

But `I` is very clearly being used:

``` cpp
    template <precision P>
    struct compute_faceforward<float, P, tvec4, true>
    {
        GLM_FUNC_QUALIFIER static tvec4<float, P> call(tvec4<float, P> const& N, tvec4<float, P> const& I, tvec4<float, P> const& Nref)
        {
            tvec4<float, P> result(uninitialize);
            result.data = glm_vec4_faceforward(N.data. I.data, Nref.data);
            return result;
        }
    };
```

If I remove `I` from the parameter list (but leave it in the function body):

``` cpp
    template <precision P>
    struct compute_faceforward<float, P, tvec4, true>
    {
-       GLM_FUNC_QUALIFIER static tvec4<float, P> call(tvec4<float, P> const& N, tvec4<float, P> const& I, tvec4<float, P> const& Nref)
+       GLM_FUNC_QUALIFIER static tvec4<float, P> call(tvec4<float, P> const& N, tvec4<float, P> const&, tvec4<float, P> const& Nref)
        {
            tvec4<float, P> result(uninitialize);
            result.data = glm_vec4_faceforward(N.data. I.data, Nref.data);
            return result;
        }
    };
```

... it compiles fine, even though `I` should now be undefined for `I.data`... Definitely one of the weirdest compiler warnings I've ever come across. Any idea what's going on here?
